WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
easy for peers to spot. The ethical lines
were stark and definitive.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.460
This chart defines historical violations.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:12.320
On the dark crimson side, we see
undeniable failures, data fabrication,

4
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.800
falsification, and overt plagiarism.

5
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.460
But modern researchers face a much more
dangerous frontier.

6
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.200
As we move across the gradient, we enter
the highly ambiguous gray areas.

7
00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.040
One prevalent example is salami slicing.

8
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.540
This occurs when a researcher takes a
single, cohesive study and divides the

9
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.360
data into multiple fragmented papers
simply to inflate their publication

10
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.860
count. Other critical violations in this
zone include selective reporting,

11
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.460
where researchers publish only the data
that supports their hypothesis,

12
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.520
and inadequate authorship, where contributors
who don't meet the criteria are

13
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.240
incorrectly credited. To resolve these
ambiguities, institutions rely on

14
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.020
global guidance...
